      <div>
        <h2>A documented risk on two fronts for fire departments</h2>
        <p class="partner-section__lead">Exertional heat stroke (EHS) affects fire and rescue services in two distinct ways. First, firefighters themselves, exposed to intense physical effort in full PPE, both on calls and during training. Second, the victims they respond to during patient care missions, particularly during periods of extreme heat.</p>
        <p class="partner-section__lead">For a complete breakdown of the physiological mechanisms and warning signs, read our <a href="https://kollder.com/blog/en/2026-06-04-exertional-heat-stroke-recognise-and-treat/" class="inline-link">guide to exertional heat stroke</a> and our season outlook on <a href="https://kollder.com/blog/en/2026-05-20-wildfire-season-2026-protecting-firefighters-heat-stroke/" class="inline-link">protecting firefighters in 2026</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="partner-narrative__side">
        <div class="partner-callout">
          <div class="partner-callout__row">
            <div class="partner-callout__figure">50%+</div>
            <div class="partner-callout__label">EHS mortality without proper cooling</div>
          </div>
          <div class="partner-callout__row">
            <div class="partner-callout__figure">30 min</div>
            <div class="partner-callout__label">Critical window to bring core temperature below 40°C</div>
          </div>
          <div class="partner-callout__row">
            <div class="partner-callout__figure">→ 0</div>
            <div class="partner-callout__label">Mortality with a correctly applied immersion protocol</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
